      "writtenOn": "2026-10-30T23:01:18Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Thanks for checking the cross-page-size case! As far as I can see, though, a 4KB process\u0027s `mm-\u003etask_size` is still `TASK_SIZE_64`, not `TASK_SIZE_4KB`, so this check doesn\u0027t reject a native handler\u0027s buffer above 512GB:\n\n`mm-\u003etask_size` is only set in `setup_new_exec()` (`me-\u003emm-\u003etask_size \u003d TASK_SIZE;`) and copied on fork. `fs/exec.c` doesn\u0027t include `\u003clinux/p3s_user_pages.h\u003e`, so that `TASK_SIZE` is arm64\u0027s own, `TASK_SIZE_64`, for 4KB and 16KB processes alike.\n\n`TASK_SIZE_4KB` (aosp/4312458) is only returned by `mm_task_size()` (aosp/4312461), behind the `TASK_SIZE` macro in the files that include `\u003clinux/p3s_user_pages.h\u003e` (aosp/4312462). Nothing in our series or in Kelvin\u0027s stores it in `mm-\u003etask_size`.\n\n`validate_unaligned_range()` reads the `mm-\u003etask_size` field, not the macro. `P3S_CONTEXT_REMOTE_MM(ctx-\u003emm)` only changes what `PAGE_SIZE` and `PAGE_MASK` evaluate to, so here it only affects the `len` alignment check.\n\nSo for any 64-bit handler, `ctx-\u003emm-\u003etask_size` and `current-\u003emm-\u003etask_size` are the same, and ART\u0027s GC serves its faults in-process anyway. The source check is upstream code that this patch doesn\u0027t touch, and aosp/4367947 only changes the result for AArch32 tasks. Since this patch doesn\u0027t need it, I\u0027d prefer to keep it out of this one. I\u0027ve also asked Kelvin on aosp/4367947 whether he can drop it.",
